To command a ₹20 LPA (₹1.3L/month) salary in Bangalore with your specific stack, you need to transition from a "Developer who knows APIs" to an "Engineer who designs Systems."At this level, interviewers focus less on syntax and more on Architecture, Scalability, and AI Orchestration.Here is the complete syllabus broken down by competency.Phase 1: The "Senior" Spring Boot Backend (40% of Interview)At 20 LPA, you are expected to handle scale and complex distributed systems, not just CRUD.Microservices Patterns:Inter-service Communication: Feign Client, RestTemplate vs. WebClient.Resilience: Circuit Breakers (Resilience4j), Rate Limiting, Retry mechanisms.Data Consistency: SAGA Pattern (Choreography vs. Orchestration), Two-Phase Commit (2PC).Advanced Spring Boot:Internals: Bean Lifecycle, Custom Auto-Configuration, AOP (Aspect Oriented Programming) for logging/security.Performance: Optimizing startup time, Profiling (JProfiler), Memory Leak detection.Security: Spring Security (JWT flows, OAuth2, Role-based access), securing Actuator endpoints.Database & Caching:JPA/Hibernate: N+1 problem, Optimistic vs. Pessimistic Locking, Second-level cache.Caching Strategy: Redis/Hazelcast implementation, Cache-Aside vs. Write-Through patterns.Database Scaling: Sharding, Read Replicas, Partitioning.Phase 2: The "AI Agent" Specialist (30% of Interview)This is your "Value Multiplier." Companies will hire you because you can build agents that actually DO work, not just chat.Orchestration Frameworks:LangChain & LangGraph: Building stateful agents (loops, memory, persistence).CrewAI / AutoGen: Multi-agent collaboration (e.g., one agent researches, another writes code).RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation):Vector Databases: Pinecone, Weaviate, or pgvector (integrating with your Postgres DB).Advanced Retrieval: Hybrid Search (Keyword + Semantic), Re-ranking results for accuracy, Parent-Document retrieval.Chunking Strategies: Semantic chunking vs. fixed-size chunking.Tool Use / Function Calling:Teaching LLMs to call your Spring Boot APIs.Defining structured schemas (Pydantic/JSON) for reliable tool execution.Deployment & LLMOps:Serving models (vLLM, Ollama) vs. API (OpenAI/Anthropic).Evaluation: Using frameworks like Ragas or LangSmith to test agent accuracy (crucial for production).Phase 3: React & Frontend Architecture (15% of Interview)You don't need to be a UI designer, but you must know how to handle complex state and AI streaming.State Management: Context API vs. Redux Toolkit vs. Zustand (preferred for simplicity).Performance: useMemo, useCallback (knowing when to use them), Virtualization (for long lists), Lazy Loading.AI Integration UI:Streaming Responses: Handling Server-Sent Events (SSE) or WebSockets for real-time AI typing effects.Optimistic UI: Showing updates before the backend confirms (for faster feel).Phase 4: System Design & Infrastructure (15% of Interview)The gatekeeper for 20 LPA roles. You must be able to draw the entire system.High-Level Design (HLD):Designing a URL Shortener, Notification Service, or Chat System.Load Balancing (Nginx, AWS ALB).Message Queues: Kafka vs. RabbitMQ (When to use which?).Low-Level Design (LLD):Design Patterns: Strategy, Factory, Observer, Builder.Schema Design: SQL vs. NoSQL choices.Docker & Kubernetes: Writing Dockerfiles for multi-stage builds (Java/Python), basic K8s concepts (Pods, Services, Ingress).The "Bridge" Project to BuildTo prove you are worth ₹20 LPA, build ONE project that connects all these pieces. Do not build a To-Do list.Project Idea: "AI-Powered Customer Support Agent"React Frontend: Chat interface that supports streaming text and rendering charts.Spring Boot Backend: Manages User Auth (JWT), Order History (Postgres), and acts as the "Tool" provider.Python AI Service:Receives query: "Where is my order #123?"Agent Logic: Decides it needs to check order status.Tool Call: Calls the Spring Boot API /orders/123.Synthesis: Generates response: "Your order is in transit."Infrastructure: Both services containerized (Docker) and communicating via REST or gRPC.
